# 🔴 `pnpm install` may NEVER silently re-resolve the dependency tree.
#
# WHY (2026-08-01, `_specs/nodepod-util-parity_plan.md` "THIRD DEFECT"). Adopting ONE new version of
# ONE package left `package.json` and `pnpm-lock.yaml` disagreeing for a moment. The next bare
# `pnpm install` did not resolve that one specifier — it re-resolved EVERY caret range in the project:
# 590 resolutions, a 9,392-line lockfile diff, `@types/node` 24→26, vite 5.4.19→5.4.21, the whole
# CodeMirror set. One of them, `react-icons` 5.5.0 → 5.7.0, dropped an export the app imports by name,
# and the ENTIRE PRODUCT white-screened.
#
# Nothing in the gates catches that: typecheck passes (the icon packages still declare the symbol),
# lint passes, and 4,589 tests passed while the app was a blank page. The only signal was the browser.
#
# `frozen-lockfile` turns that silent mass upgrade into an immediate, readable refusal naming the
# mismatch. It only governs `pnpm install`:
#
#   pnpm install            → installs EXACTLY the lockfile; refuses if package.json disagrees
#   pnpm add <pkg>          → works normally, updates both files together
#   pnpm update <pkg>       → works normally, deliberate and scoped
#
# So the ordinary "just install the repo" path is safe by default, and every path that CHANGES
# dependencies stays explicit. If a refusal is legitimate (you edited package.json on purpose), run
# `pnpm install --no-frozen-lockfile` — and then READ `git diff --stat pnpm-lock.yaml` before
# committing. A one-package change is a handful of lines; thousands means the tree was re-resolved.
frozen-lockfile=true
